    "chapter_title": "Structure Of Atom",
    "section": "Subatomic particles: Dalton's atomic theory was able to explain the laws of chemical combination successfully. However, it failed to explain some properties of matter. For example, it could not explain why substances like glass or ebonite when rubbed with silk or fur, generate electricity. Discovery of subatomic particles in late nineteenth and early twentieth century set a blow to Dalton's atomic model of hard sphere. Three important subatomic particles, namely, proton, electron and neutron which are of concern to Chemistry were discovered. Proton and neutron are present in the atomic nucleus and together are called nucleons. Electrons are present in the extranuclear part of an atom. The properties of electron, proton and neutron are summarised in Table 4.1",
